# SitGlow project rules

You are building **SitGlow**, a browser-based, on-device webcam posture and desk-health PWA for US consumers and teams. Tagline: **"Sit better. Feel brighter."**

## Source of truth
- Specs live in `docs/`: `product-spec.md` (screens + acceptance criteria), `posture-engine.md` (scoring algorithm), `brand.md` (colors, type, voice), `landing-page.md` (marketing copy).
- Visual targets live in `design/` (see `design/README.md`). Match layout, hierarchy and mood of the mockups. If a mockup shows the old name "Desk Habits" or UK wording, ignore that; SitGlow branding and these rules win.
- If a spec and a mockup disagree, follow the spec and mention the difference.

## Brand
- Background: crisp white `#FFFFFF`; surfaces `#F8FAF9`; text `#0F172A` / muted `#475569`.
- Primary emerald: `#047857` (buttons, headings accents), lighter emerald `#10B981` (progress, success).
- Warm amber glow accent: `#F59E0B` (the "glow": score rings, logo sun, highlights). Use sparingly.
- Sky-blue accent: `#38BDF8` (info, water/break reminders, charts secondary).
- Font: Inter (fallback system-ui sans-serif). Rounded corners (`rounded-2xl` cards), soft shadows, generous whitespace.
- Imagery: real photos of people in sunlit offices and nature. Professional and bright, never cartoonish.
- Logo: a seated figure whose head is a glowing amber sun.
- Define brand colors as Tailwind theme tokens (`emerald-brand`, `glow`, `sky-brand`, etc.); never hard-code hex values in components.

## Tone and wording
- Warm, encouraging, short. Coach, not nag. Never shame the user.
- **US English and US context only.** Never mention UK, EU, GDPR, or DSE, and avoid UK spellings (use "color", "personalize"). Call it a "desk setup check" and "workplace ergonomics programs". Measure in inches.
- Units: inches, imperial. Dates: US format.
- **No medical claims.** Say "fewer aches", "feel better at your desk", "build healthier habits". Never "cure", "treat", "fix back pain", "diagnose", "prevent injury". Show "SitGlow is a wellness tool, not a medical device" where posture data is explained.

## Privacy (non-negotiable)
- Camera frames are processed **only in the browser**. Never upload, stream, store, or log video frames, images, or raw landmarks to any server.
- **No network calls may include camera-derived data.** The only data persisted is derived numbers (scores, baseline numbers, settings, streaks), stored locally in IndexedDB/localStorage.
- No analytics SDKs, trackers, or third-party scripts that could access the camera. MediaPipe model/WASM files should be self-hosted in `public/` (or loaded from a pinned CDN for dev only, with a TODO to self-host).
- Always show a visible camera-on indicator and a one-click Pause camera. Provide "Delete all my data".
- Stop all camera tracks when the user pauses, leaves, or the app is hidden for long periods.

## Tech stack
- Vite + React 18+ + TypeScript (`strict: true`) + Tailwind CSS.
- PWA via `vite-plugin-pwa`.
- `@mediapipe/tasks-vision`: PoseLandmarker + FaceLandmarker (iris), `runningMode: 'VIDEO'`, GPU delegate with CPU fallback.
- Routing: `react-router-dom`. State: React hooks/context (or Zustand if it grows). Storage: `idb-keyval` or a tiny IndexedDB wrapper.
- Tests: Vitest + React Testing Library for logic (posture engine must have unit tests).

## Code style
- TypeScript strict; no `any` (use `unknown` + narrowing). Explicit types on public functions.
- Small, focused components (< ~150 lines). Pure logic (posture engine, scoring, streaks) lives in `src/lib/` with no React imports, so it is unit-testable.
- Folder layout: `src/app` (shell, routes), `src/features/<feature>`, `src/components` (shared UI), `src/lib` (pure logic), `src/hooks`, `src/styles`.
- Accessible by default: semantic HTML, labels on all controls, visible focus rings, keyboard navigable, `aria-live="polite"` for score/nudge updates, color is never the only signal, respects `prefers-reduced-motion`, WCAG AA contrast.
- Performance: run inference with `requestAnimationFrame` / `requestVideoFrameCallback`, throttle to ~15 fps when visible and ~2-5 fps in background/PiP; never block the UI thread with heavy work.
- Keep tunable numbers (thresholds, weights) in one config file (`src/lib/posture/config.ts`).
- Leave clear `// TODO:` comments for anything stubbed (e.g., waitlist backend).

## Workflow
- Work in the step order of `prompts/`. Don't build future phases early unless asked.
- After each step, make sure `npm run dev`, `npm run build`, and `npm test` (once tests exist) pass, and tell the user how to verify in plain language.
